# ---------------------------------------------------------------- secrets ---
# Anything holding a real password. The *.example / committed *.env templates
# stay tracked: `.env` matches only the exact filename, and config.env is
# deliberately not covered by these patterns.
.env
config.local.env
web/.env

# ------------------------------------------------------------ big binaries ---
# Reverse-engineering inputs. Kept locally, never committed — the APK alone is
# 74 MB (past GitHub's 50 MB warning) and the pcap contains the camera's live
# traffic, password included. Re-fetch the APK from APKPure; re-capture the pcap
# with PCAPdroid if ever needed. What they taught us is written up in README.md.
*.apk
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.dex
*.so

# Decompiler / analysis output (jadx, Ghidra, scratch dumps)
jadxout/
scratchpad/
*.gpr
*.rep

# --------------------------------------------------------- captured video ---
# Stream dumps and recordings — regenerated on every run, and large.
*.h264
*.h265
*.hevc
*.mp4
*.m3u8
*.m4s
recordings/
vps/docker/recordings/

# Assignment reader's runtime state: state.json, the last frame, and the
# archive of previous versions. Regenerated on every run.
assignment/data/
# NB: no bare `*.ts` here, however tempting for MPEG-TS dumps — it also matches
# TypeScript, and would silently drop web/server.ts from the repo (it did once).
# MPEG-TS only ever lands in the ignored recordings/ dirs anyway.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- logs ---
*.log
session-*.log

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- python ---
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- node ---
node_modules/

# ------------------------------------------------------- editors / OS junk ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
